          ],
          "content": "Curiosity | Textbook of Science | Grade 7"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.085,
            0.755,
            0.161
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "5.2 A Substance May Change in Appearance and Not Remain the Same!"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.274,
            0.192,
            0.54,
            0.213
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 5.3: Let us explore"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.267,
            0.226,
            0.883,
            0.263
          ],
          "content": "Take two glass tumblers or small transparent bottles. Mark them A and B."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.267,
            0.269,
            0.882,
            0.307
          ],
          "content": "Fill one-fourth of glass tumbler A with tap water and one-fourth of glass tumbler B with lime water."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.267,
            0.311,
            0.882,
            0.35
          ],
          "content": "Now, blow air (exhale) into each glass tumbler, one at a time, using separate straws (Fig. 5.2) and observe them."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.219,
            0.345,
            0.258,
            0.371
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.257,
            0.352,
            0.829,
            0.373
          ],
          "content": "**Caution** — Do not suck the water or lime water while doing this."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.143,
            0.381,
            0.877,
            0.579
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.295,
            0.591,
            0.706,
            0.609
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 5.2: Blowing air in (a) tap water; (b) lime water"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.287,
            0.612,
            0.56,
            0.632
          ],
          "content": "Do you notice any changes?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.633,
            0.885,
            0.93
          ],
          "content": "In glass tumbler A, blowing air into water creates bubbles only, and there is no change in the appearance of the water. In glass tumbler B, blowing air into lime water creates bubbles, and turns the lime water milky (or cloudy). If we leave it for some time, a white substance settles at the bottom of the glass tumbler, indicating something new has formed. Such changes, in which one or more new substances are formed, are called **chemical changes**. New substances are formed through a process called **chemical reaction**. In this case, carbon dioxide from the air you breathe out (exhale) reacts with lime water, and forms a new white-coloured substance (calcium carbonate) that is insoluble in water. Therefore, the liquid in the bottle appears milky. Along with this a small amount of water is also formed. This formation of a new substance indicates a chemical change. The chemical reaction involved in this change can be represented in short form as a chemical equation. 1"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.012,
